Hello 3DPrint.com team,

a story lead built around a single, visual idea.

A downloaded STL of a knurled handle contains no knurl. It contains a few
hundred triangles that happen to look like one. That is why no tool lets you
make the grooves deeper: there is nothing to change, only geometry to redraw.

Solidon3D 0.5.0 recognises the pattern itself and presents it as one feature
with pitch, cell width and depth. One click removes it, three numbers set it
again, and the cells stay where they were. Around a cylinder the pattern is
measured in the unrolled surface, so the grooves are equally deep everywhere
and the circumference closes without a seam.

For context, since the category is hard to place: Solidon3D sits between the
downloaded model and the slicer. Click a hole and its dimensions appear on the
model, type 8 instead of 6 and the countersink follows, pick a fit for the
screw, read the check report, hand the file to the slicer you already use. It
is built for the most common job in 3D printing, adapting a downloaded model
that almost fits, and there is currently no good tool for that. CAD packages
accept an STL but cannot grip triangles. The mesh tools that could edit them
are discontinued, Meshmixer and Microsoft's 3D Builder. Parametric customisers
need an author who anticipated the parameter.

The structural change behind the pattern work: the mesh becomes an exact body.
Holes, countersinks and threads stay real faces and edges through moving,
duplicating and removing, and STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly, so the
result can be carried into a CAD system instead of arriving as triangles
again. The options that used to switch between the mesh and the exact kernel
are gone.

Other items worth a line: an existing thread gets its counterpart on the
mating part at the press of a button, in a table size and as a fit; resin
printers are supported with pixel size and minimum wall; and a model with open
areas is closed while it is read.

The demo is free and complete, so you can try any of this straight away.
Print-resolution images and the models I used are available on request.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, in six languages, with no account
and no network.

